doc.para:
with Statement
　The with statement specifies the object to reference when the left side (object) of the . (dot) operator is omitted.

　The syntax is as follows:

with(expression)
	statement or block


　When a . (dot) operator with its left side omitted is used within "statement or block", the object referenced by that . (dot) operator will be the object specified by expression in the with statement.


	with(obj) .member = 1; // Same as obj.member = 1;

	with(obj)
	{
		.member1 = 1; // Assign 1 to obj.member1
		.member2 = 2; // Assign 2 to obj.member2
		.member3++; // Increment obj.member3
		.method(); // Call obj.method
	}


　This statement can reduce typing, especially when setting a large number of properties on an object or calling methods consecutively.

　For example,


	var object = new Foo();
	object.setPos(0, 0);
	object.setSize(100, 100);
	object.name = "Unnamed";
	object.color = 0xffffffff;


　A script like this can be written as follows:


	var object = new Foo();
	with(object)
	{
		.setPos(0, 0);
		.setSize(100, 100);
		.name = "Unnamed";
		.color = 0xffffffff;
	}

with Statement and Objects
　The expression specified in the with statement is evaluated only once at the beginning when with is executed, and thereafter it is merely referenced.

　For example,


this.dic = %[];
with(this.dic)
{
	.member = 1; // Assign 1 to this.dic.member
	this.dic = 0; // Assign something else to dic
	.member = 2; // Assign 2 to this.dic.member
}


　Even if you write it like this, the second assignment to .member will succeed. This is because this.dic is evaluated in the initial state when with is executed, and that evaluated result is used from then on. It does not evaluate this.dic every time.

　Since evaluation occurs only the first time, in use cases where an object is referenced multiple times, code to reference the object is not generated every time, which may improve performance.

　The above example can be thought of as generating code equivalent to the following:


this.dic = %[];
{
	var anonymous_special_local_variable = this.dic;
	anonymous_special_local_variable.member = 1;
	this.dic = 0;
	anonymous_special_local_variable.member = 2;
}


　In the with statement, the result of evaluating expression is assigned to an anonymous special local variable that can only be accessed by the . (dot) operator with its left side omitted. Thereafter, that "anonymous special local variable" will be referenced by the . (dot) operator with its left side omitted.
　The scope in which the result of evaluating expression is held can also be considered the same as the local variable scope of the above "anonymous special local variable" (and the scope affected by the with statement corresponds to this as well).

The . Operator Outside the with Statement When the left side of the . (dot) operator is omitted outside a with statement, it references the global object. For example, if you write
.foo = 1;
outside a with statement, it
global.foo = 1;
has the same meaning as.
